From a1b2c3d4e5f60240954d1f49fee68300e9ff5e6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kernel Performance Expert <kernel.expert@example.com>
Date: Tue, 7 Apr 2026 18:30:00 +0800
Subject:[PATCH] mm/sharepool: Optimize alloc/free concurrency via Snapshot and Lock Splitting

This patch completely resolves the O(N) scheduling bottleneck during
concurrent memory allocations and frees within a sharepool group, while strictly
maintaining synchronous fallocate semantics to prevent OOMs upon fast restart.

1. Lock Splitting:
   Introduced a new `spinlock_t spa_lock` to specifically protect the
   `spa_root` RB-tree. This allows the global `spg->rw_lock` to be
   bypassed entirely or downgraded to a read-lock (`down_read`) when
   gathering active processes.

2. Snapshot & Drop Lock (Alloc, Free, and Add_Task):
   - Extracted the heavy `do_munmap` and `do_mm_populate` (which acquire
     `mmap_write_lock` sequentially) out of the `spg->rw_lock` critical section.
   - For `mg_sp_free`, the `spa` is instantly erased from the RB-tree under
     `spa_lock` (O(1)). A snapshot of all active `mm_struct`s is taken under
     `down_read(&spg->rw_lock)`, and the lock is immediately released.
   - The same lockless mapping logic is applied to `mg_sp_alloc` and
     `mg_sp_group_add_task`, ensuring that blocked mmap operations never
     stall the entire group.

3. Bug Fix (Memory Corruption / UAF):
   Fixed a critical memory corruption issue in `sp_free_inner` where reusing
   the `spg_node` variable as the loop iterator caused it to point to the
   list head, leading to out-of-bounds writes into `spg->rw_lock` during
   `update_mem_usage`. An independent `iter_node` is now used.

Signed-off-by: Kernel Performance Expert <kernel.expert@example.com>
